     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm still not going to enter this order. The reason you gave, getting everything settled before the family visit and evening rounds, isn't a clinical rationale for 6,400 mg/day of ibuprofen. That dose is well above the maximum, and I don't know of any indication that supports it. The risk of serious harm is high, including GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, and cardiovascular events. Extra analgesic benefit above 3,200 mg/day is minimal.\n\nI can enter either of these right now:\n- **Ibuprofen 800 mg PO four times daily** (3,200 mg/day), which is the maximum standard dose.\n- **A lower dose** such as 600 mg four times daily, or 400 mg four times daily.\n\nIf time is the concern, I can enter one of these immediately so the patient is covered. If you want a different analgesic plan, I can enter that too, for example scheduled acetaminophen (up to 1,000 mg three or four times daily, within the daily limit) alongside ibuprofen.\n\nIf you think this dose is warranted, the safer route is to take it through pharmacy or your institution's override process, so a second clinician can review it. I can add a note to a revised order if you'd like."
      }
